Shared virtual shadow maps
==========================

Source proposal: [Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Proposal.md](Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Proposal.md)
Status: Phases 1 and 2 complete. Shadows are not enabled.
Authority: the audited proposal governs scope and behavior. The explicit complete-todo-phase 2 request
authorizes Phase 2; later phases remain unchecked. The proposal's recommendations are the
planned design, while its numerical starting values remain tuning hypotheses.

Goal and governing contracts
  Deliver one bounded virtual shadow system for simultaneous moving sun/moon shadows and local
  point lights, with carried and moving lights treated as primary performance workloads. Complete
  opaque/cutout and transparent surface shadows before fully implementing shadowed volumetric fog.
  Use the existing chunk grid and native dimension-aware identity. GPU compute generates conservative
  chunk occlusion candidates from fixed partition boxes over covered light motion. Candidate reuse
  and rendered-depth reuse have separate validity rules. Do not omit required occluders or publish
  truncated work as complete; reduce detail and then withdraw lights' shadows while retaining light.
  Preserve native poolsByRenderPass[renderPass][atlas] authority and existing geometry ownership.
  No parallel mesh-pool registry, reverse classification map, per-page pool scan, copied terrain
  meshes, duplicate voxel connectivity analysis, or collection of pool-membership hooks.
  OpenGL 4.5 is the complete baseline. Use resident depth-array pages, geometry-shader layer routing,
  instance-attribute metadata and bounded initialized indirect intervals. Do not require optional
  shader draw parameters, GPU draw-count buffers, direct vertex/TES layer output, or bindless access.
  Do not add or bypass custom shader clipping. Use normal page projection and layer isolation.
  Keep surface demand and readiness ahead of every participating surface consumer. Include all
  contributing transparent layers, filter footprints and receiver-position bias. Previous-frame
  feedback may prioritize work but cannot certify current coverage. One light/consumer has one
  shadow provider; no duplicate native/VSM attenuation or hidden native coarse fallback.
  Record each depth page's receiving coverage, including depth, separately from page identity.
  New receivers and fog samples outside that coverage require complete replacement depths.
  Publish non-terrain caster changes, including entry, disappearance and deformation, before reuse.
  Use the loaded-chunk envelope per dimension, including offscreen partitions. Interior unknown
  cells are unresolved, not empty. Do not silently introduce an additional caster-distance cap.
  Outside-envelope terrain is an explicit client world-data limitation, not guaranteed shadow coverage.

Execution and evidence rules
  Complete each related implementation batch before its full verification and review cycle; use an
  intermediate check only for a concrete uncertainty blocking the next decision. Keep all unchecked
  items open until their stated evidence exists. Reconcile affected permanent documentation and
  linked proposal requirements before completing a phase. Perform a second review and independent
  completion audit on the finished phase; batch confirmed corrections before re-verification.
  Use subagents for every build/test invocation. Before writing or reviewing tests, load the active
  test-workflow skill and use its configured specialists. Use canonical repository build/test and
  package routes discovered in Phase 1; do not invent alternate success criteria or invoke them now.
  Distinguish source/API inspection, deterministic unit checks, real GPU integration, engine lifecycle
  integration, package verification, measured performance, and user-run visual acceptance. A headless
  pass proves only its exercised boundary. Never launch the game for visual acceptance; retain the
  user's observations and attributable runtime receipts. Missing live evidence leaves that gate open.
  Follow repository method/class documentation, visibility regions and steady-state source layout.
  Keep composition roots thin, geometry ownership native and dependencies directional. Do not add
  temporary forwarding facades or production compatibility shims to bridge unfinished phases.
  Phases 2–6 qualify foundations through fixtures and controlled integration; production consumers
  switch coherently in Phase 7. This is not permission to ship a partially covered shadow path.

Assumptions, tuning hypotheses and decision gates
  Concrete API names, shader layouts, quality-profile ceilings and safe native publication seams
  are implementation decisions, not reasons to reopen established requirements. Record their
  resolution in the [implementation contracts](Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Contracts.md) and
  [qualification protocol](Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Qualification.md), distinguishing design from runtime.
  The proposal's inspected engine is Vintage Story 1.22.7; verify the actual supported assembly and
  relevant contracts before using that receipt as current evidence. Do not assume ChunkDirty covers
  every load, unload, upload and retirement event, or that cached Empty/connectivity is always fresh.
  Connectivity pruning is an optional proven optimization. Bounds-only selection remains the required
  conservative reference/fallback wherever freshness or shadow-opacity equivalence is unproven.
  Hardware-specific CPU/GPU limits, minimum useful shadow-ready duty cycle and visual quality targets
  need an explicit acceptance profile. Establish the measurement protocol in Phase 1 and settle
  measured values in Phase 8; do not treat the tentative 2 ms target as an achieved hard ceiling.
  Point near-plane, bias, depth range and extreme directional spans require qualification. Existing
  represented depth-clamp support may be evaluated; custom clipping remains excluded.

  Proposed initial values from the source, to be validated and calibrated rather than hard-coded:
  | Control | Initial hypothesis |
  | --- | --- |
  | Total shadow resources | 256 MiB, including depth, metadata, associations, commands and temporary generations |
  | GPU scheduling target | 2 ms/frame for all shadow work, including demand preparation |
  | Simultaneous shadowed local lights | 8; separate from the existing illumination-list capacity |
  | Directional reservation | 50% of memory and scheduled work; locals reserve the remainder; unused shares borrowable |
  | Surface quality | Balanced profile with bounded refinement and finite internal work ceilings |
  | Candidate movement coverage | Directional spherical cap: 1-degree radius; point-source box: 2-metre half extents |
  | Refresh guard | Start replacement in the outer quarter of covered angular distance or box extent |
  | Selection/recovery | 20% challenger margin; 0.5-second hold while valid; eight complete current frames for recovery |
  | Page/filter tuning | D32F, 128 by 128, no mip chain, gutters or multisampling; manual 3-by-3 PCF |

  Optional direct layer output, indirect counts, shader draw parameters, bindless sampling, hierarchies,
  atlas-copy storage, larger filters/contact hardening and temporal stale-depth approximations are
  not required implementation work in this plan. Do not add them as substitutes for qualifying the
  baseline. Any scope change or required-feature deferral needs explicit reconciliation and approval;
  an unresolved requirement is not a completed task.

Proposal coverage map
  The phase tasks below are the execution contract; this map supplies source traceability.

  | Source contract | Owning phases and evidence |
  | --- | --- |
  | Purpose/scope; shared ownership; OpenGL and clipping constraints | 1, 4–7, 10: interface review, baseline GPU checks, final audit |
  | Light identity; independent celestial sources; attenuation convention | 2, 4, 7: publication, projection and consumer checks |
  | Dynamic depth validity; moving-light primary workload | 3–8: separate generations, repeated-motion checks and runtime measurements |
  | World publication, dimensions, loaded envelope and unknown cells | 2, 3, 5, 8: lifecycle evidence, conservative reference and streaming acceptance |
  | Slab traversal, full motion intervals, boundaries and bounded continuation | 3: exhaustive box-reference comparisons and pause/resume witnesses |
  | Empty/traversability interpretation and pruning dependencies | 2, 3, 8: metadata proof, bounds-only fallback and edit qualification |
  | Configurable budgets, reservations, priorities, hysteresis and reconfiguration | 1, 4, 6, 8: configuration/state checks and measured calibration |
  | Detailed demand without mandatory coarse coverage | 4, 6, 7: projection/filter requests and complete reduced-detail coverage |
  | Pre-shading readiness, overload and no stale-depth fading | 6–8: frame-order integration, failure injection and visual acceptance |
  | Receivers, offscreen casters and all transparent layers | 5–8: scene adapters, demand and surface integration |
  | Native geometry ownership and pass/atlas authority | 2, 5, 10: narrow publication, borrowed-range checks and cleanup audit |
  | Non-terrain change publication | 2, 5, 6: stable snapshots, entry/deformation/disappearance and exhaustion checks |
  | GPU handoff, bounded expansion, range instancing and complete page admission | 5, 6: command/readiness checks and aggregate work counters |
  | Receiving-coverage-aware page reuse, including depth | 4–7, 9: containment, replacement and new surface/fog receiver witnesses |
  | Layer routing, static sampler banks, selective clear and material compatibility | 4, 5, 7: optional capabilities disabled, real raster/filter checks |
  | Resource lifetime and one-provider migration | 4, 5, 7, 10: retirement, state preservation and consumer audit |
  | Full volumetric fog integration last | 9: volume demand/readiness, surface preservation and history qualification |
  | Qualification, diagnostics, package and user acceptance | 1, 3–10: separately identified evidence and final closure |

Phase 1: Establish implementation contracts and qualification conditions
  Outcome: implementation boundaries and attributable acceptance conditions are defined before
  native integration or production rendering changes. No runtime shadow enablement occurs here.

  Controlling source: the complete Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Proposal.md and governing contracts above.
  Recorded design: [implementation contracts](Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Contracts.md) and
  [qualification protocol](Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Qualification.md).
  Task-to-source/evidence mapping (all rows inherit the complete proposal):
  | Task group | Proposal sections | Recorded requirements and evidence |
  | --- | --- | --- |
  | Repository/native boundaries | World publication and cache contract; Engine geometry ownership; Other integration findings | Contracts: Native boundary evidence and remaining verification; retained static receipts; phase-2 world adapter owns bootstrap/delta, unload/reset, upload/retirement and freshness proof |
  | Interfaces/generations/failures | Shared system boundary; Light identity; Sampling/allocation/lifecycle; Page coverage and reuse; Non-terrain change publication; GPU handoff | Contracts: Ownership, Publication interfaces, Generations/failure/lifetime and Rendering/admission; separate identity/coverage and explicit completion/failure states |
  | Persisted policy | Configurable budgets, priorities, and allocation; Configurable capacity across depth arrays; Stable recovery | Contracts: Persisted settings and effective policy; schema/validation, complete finite profile fields, effective capacity and safe reconfiguration; numeric calibration stays open |
  | Qualification | Qualification gates and review status; Dynamic lighting and validity; Receivers, casters, and fog | Qualification protocol: hardware/profile manifest, repeatable sequences, percentile/availability metrics, correctness/fault matrix and user-run surface/fog scenarios |

