Phase 3: Generate and reuse conservative GPU chunk occlusion candidates
  Depends on Phase 2.
  Outcome: GPU associations are conservatively complete, resumable and reusable across covered light
  movement independently of depth updates. No triangle/mesh-AABB inspection is used for terrain search.

  ☐ Implement the proposal's chunk-grid slab sweep for directional caps and point-source boxes.
    ☐ For directional coverage, choose fixed-sign bounded axial components, partition across covering
      signed dominant-axis sectors when necessary and conservatively bound the full cap interior.
    ☐ Use the stated hMin/hMax and transverse slope interval products; enumerate every intersected slab
      to the declared domain boundary, including low sun angles, rather than following a center ray.
    ☐ For point lights, bound the receiver/source segment hull, solve each slab's linear u inequalities
      over [0,1], and use transverse endpoint extrema. Include receiver/source cells and overlap cases;
      do not extend paths infinitely beyond the source or drop eligible receivers using another radius.
    ☐ Apply closed-boundary cell coverage, outward numerical bounds, negative coordinates, signed-axis
      reflection and initial/final slab rules. Do not substitute corner rays or an arbitrary inward epsilon.
  ☐ Implement bounded headers, segmented coordinate lists, deduplication and continuation storage.
    ☐ Bound slab advances, cell visits and output separately; retain subset/slab/transverse cursor and
      input generation. Revalidate coverage/dependencies before resuming or publishing.
    ☐ Publish only complete deduplicated results. Charge old and replacement generations to the same
      memory budget; exhausted output remains incomplete rather than shortening the search horizon.
    ☐ Refresh within the configured guard; retain valid associations during depth updates and expire
      them when motion/influence/domain coverage or relevant dependencies no longer hold.
  ☐ Apply bounded GPU changed-partition checks to active covered regions and pruning dependencies.
    ☐ Retain coordinates in bounds-only lists and evaluate current geometry/emptiness at consumption.
      If empty coordinates are omitted, record emptiness dependencies and invalidate on change or lost proof.
    ☐ Publish existing face-pair connectivity/freshness without another voxel scan. Do not call it an
      exact solid flag or straight-through hole test; stale/unavailable connectivity stays potentially open.
    ☐ Qualify opacity equivalence and conservative path coverage before enabling connectivity pruning.
      Retain blocking chunks, handle source/receiver-inside cases and fall back to bounds-only selection
      wherever pruning is unproven. Opening passages must invalidate affected pruned associations.
  ☐ Delegate deterministic CPU-reference and real GPU comparisons over bounded exhaustive chunk-box
    domains: cap interiors/sectors, near-horizontal directions, negative/boundary cases, point overlap,
    self-shadow cells, movement within/outside coverage, empty edits, streaming and pause/resume/overflow.
    ☐ Require zero missing reference occluders; record false positives, visits, deduplication and storage
      costs separately. Reference enumeration is verification tooling, not a production CPU candidate path.
  ☐ Completion: conservative GPU results and bounded failure/continuation behavior have attributable
    receipts; pruning has either passed its proof gate or is explicitly disabled in favor of bounds-only
    operation. Association reuse never certifies old depth under a changed projection.

Phase 4: Implement projections, physical pages and shared sampling
  Depends on Phases 1–3.
  Outcome: both light types can allocate, address, validate and sample depth pages using controlled GPU
  fixtures; the engine caster-rendering path is intentionally not yet integrated.

  ☐ Implement independent sun/moon orthographic clipmaps and six perspective point-light faces.
    ☐ Double world texel width between directional levels; snap placement while preserving matching
      translation reuse. Include source/lifetime, projection generation, face/level and virtual XY keys.
    ☐ Fit directional depth to represented caster coverage; invalidate depth on light-basis/point-position
      changes. Establish explicit near-plane, bias, depth-range and material-culling conventions.
  ☐ Implement bounded GPU page tables, allocation/eviction and free/retirement queues through the RHI.
    ☐ Use resident D32F array layers at the initial page dimensions with no gutters/mips/MSAA. Assign
      array/layer/generation; reject lookup/probe/allocation exhaustion instead of aliasing entries.
    ☐ Validate depth storage plus metadata/temporary resources against configured memory, layer/texture
      limits and the planned binding layouts of every participating shader. Account for array submission
      groups; Phase 7 rechecks sampler availability against the completed consumer integrations.
    ☐ Evict invalid/unreferenced entries before required coverage; never overwrite in-flight pages.
      Retain storage until GPU use completes without blocking for timing or completion readback.
    ☐ Implement disable/world leave/context loss/source or dimension removal/shader replacement and
      reconfiguration retirement. Grow incrementally, drain shrink excess visibly and avoid two full pools
      during format changes. Borrowed engine geometry remains engine-owned.
  ☐ Store rendered receiving coverage separately from page identity, including receiver depth, filter
    and bias footprints, and scene/projection/assignment dependencies.
    ☐ Bound region metadata and containment work. A conservative enclosure requires complete caster
      selection for that enclosure; extending candidate lists alone cannot extend rendered-depth validity.
    ☐ Require complete replacement for the union of currently required regions when coverage grows;
      publish depths and coverage together. Until replacement, old data covers only its certified region.
  ☐ Implement shared manual 3-by-3 PCF with explicit per-tap virtual lookup and depth comparisons.
    ☐ Use statically addressed depth-array samplers and point fetches. Remap cube-edge directions and
      reference depth to the destination face; keep directional-level samples world-consistent.
    ☐ Include every tap in demand/validity, use the shared bias convention and return diagnosed unshadowed
      visibility on unexpected invalid lookup. Do not average depths into coarse fallback levels.
  ☐ Delegate unit and real GPU checks for projection/addressing, seams, multiple arrays, invalid entries,
    assignment reuse, capacity failure, retirement, large coordinates and region containment/replacement.
    ☐ Verify receivers at different depths sharing one page and test the full sampler with optional
      capabilities disabled. Keep synthetic depth fixtures distinct from actual caster-rendering evidence.
  ☐ Completion: baseline storage/sampling and coverage-aware validity pass their scoped checks; no optional
    capability is needed, and remaining actual geometry/near-source qualification is explicitly owned by
    Phase 5 rather than claimed from synthetic pages.

Phase 5: Render complete pages from native scene geometry
  Depends on Phases 2–4.
  Outcome: selected terrain and non-terrain geometry can produce complete depth pages through the
  OpenGL 4.5 baseline without per-page CPU selection readback or mesh ownership changes.

  ☐ Implement shadow scene adapters over current native geometry publications.
    ☐ Traverse poolsByRenderPass[renderPass][atlas] once per bounded work batch for compatible groups;
      use native pool IDs and chunk ranges. Preserve engine culling draw arrays, statistics and LOD rules.
    ☐ Support ordinary indexed and shader-storage terrain layouts using borrowed buffers and shadow-specific
      vertex inputs; do not mutate native VAOs, copy meshes or treat the liquid adapter as universal.
    ☐ Include eligible receiver geometry for self-shadowing and all required offscreen casters. Preserve
      cutouts, UV/atlas sampling, warping, displacement, foliage, moving chunks and geometry detail selection.
    ☐ Prepare entity animation once per frame, including offscreen casters; reuse current preparation for
      demand and shadow passes. Preserve object instancing rather than repurposing it as terrain page instances.
  ☐ Connect actual non-terrain adapters to the Phase 2 change publication contract.
    ☐ Report creation/disappearance, prior/current bounds, motion, animation/deformation and shadow-relevant
      material/eligibility changes even with unchanged native range or object transform.
    ☐ Invalidate page depths intersecting the changed light-to-receiver regions, including newly entering
      casters absent from previous draw lists. Apply changes since each cached publication before reuse.
    ☐ Bound retained changes; invalidate conservatively before discarding unapplied changes and keep affected
      pages uncertified while processing is unfinished. Restore validity only from complete current draws.
  ☐ Implement GPU candidate-to-range/page expansion and complete page admission.
    ☐ Intersect candidate chunk boxes with page coverage, resolve eligible current ranges and deduplicate
      page/range identity plus generations using bounded insertion and prefix-sum compaction.
    ☐ Require complete associations and all current terrain/non-terrain inputs before admission. Bound input
      visits, expansion, temporary pairs, any counting/emission repeat, total groups/slots and page instances.
    ☐ Use one terrain command slot per compatible range/destination array, with selected pages as instances.
      Eight instances still incur eight geometry repetitions; account for tessellation and material costs.
    ☐ Initialize every submitted indirect slot, including inactive ones. The CPU knows bounded intervals
      from supplied geometry/groups; no GPU candidate or draw-count readback is needed on the baseline.
  ☐ Implement baseline integer instance-attribute metadata transport and geometry-shader layer output.
    ☐ Carry page metadata unchanged through vertex/tessellation stages, following final deformation. Emit
      each selected triangle once per page instance while preserving winding, positions and material outputs.
    ☐ Respect vertex-input limits and existing entity instancing semantics; do not depend on shader-visible
      draw/base-instance parameters or direct vertex/TES layer output.
    ☐ Express shader-storage, indirect-command and vertex-fetch visibility plus resource/pass lifetimes.
  ☐ Implement selective page clear using layered full-page geometry, depth writes and an always-passing
    depth test. Preserve the attachment on pass entry; never clear all cached layers with a pass clear.
    ☐ Clear only admitted replacement layers, then draw all required geometry before publishing depths and
      receiving coverage. Overflow/incomplete selection must not clear or publish a partially built page.
  ☐ Delegate real GPU raster and engine-adapter checks with optional features disabled: ordinary/SSBO and
    tessellated geometry, cutouts/deformation, offscreen entities, source-near crossings, array groups,
    untouched cached layers, stale ranges, caster entry/removal/pose changes and forced capacity failures.
  ☐ Completion: complete actual scene depth pages are demonstrated on the baseline, geometry/state ownership
    is preserved and all failure paths withhold invalid results. Measurements include repeated geometry,
    inactive command slots, preparation and clear work; no production surface completeness claim yet.

Phase 6: Integrate current-frame demand, arbitration and readiness
  Depends on Phases 2–5.
  Outcome: one bounded current-frame shadow update produces coherent per-light surface readiness before
  shading. Controlled integration proves the gate before production consumer migration in Phase 7.

  ☐ Integrate scene preparation, current light capture, surface-demand prepass, candidate/page work and
    readiness publication before the earliest participating surface draw, preserving downstream draw order.
    ☐ Place the gate after VgeLightsRenderer's current capture; do not rely on the late opaque 8.5-to-9.0
      receiver/direct-light interval for earlier forward surfaces.
    ☐ Generate current opaque/cutout, entity/foliage, water and all contributing transparent-layer requests.
      Preserve deformation, alpha/visibility rules and valid opaque-depth rejection; do not let transparent
      depth writes hide later layers. Require conservative early requests for late receiver categories.
    ☐ Include filter/bias footprints, receiving depth and light influence. Conservative projected bounds
      are allowed only with complete coverage and charged extra demand. Feedback history only prioritizes.
  ☐ Implement persisted settings and the scheduler against actual resource and work counters.
    ☐ Enforce aggregate input/traversal/expansion/group/slot/instance/draw/retry limits across the entire
      batch, not a full allowance per group or array. Include demand and entity preparation costs.
    ☐ Use directional/local reservations with borrowing and safe reclamation; retain independent sun/moon
      opportunities. Apply carried-source preference, visible contribution/cost, bounded waiting age,
      stable-ID ties, selection margins and valid-state hold times from the quality profile.
    ☐ Seek complete coverage at affordable detail before refinement, then withdraw selected lights if
      minimum acceptable detail cannot fit. Bound retries; larger coarse pages may still be too expensive.
    ☐ Use nonblocking timing feedback for later cost estimates, never as proof of completeness or an exact
      frame-time ceiling. Keep all temporary generations, coverage metadata and change processing charged.
  ☐ Reduce page validity/coverage to per-light readiness before any participating surface consumer.
    ☐ Reject unfinished demand, request overflow, unresolved geometry and invalidation work. Verify new
      receiver containment even for unchanged light/scene/page identity; replace depths when required.
    ☐ Retain illumination for unavailable lights. Never sample partial, stale or reassigned depths or
      silently switch to page-shaped local fallbacks. Keep sun/moon readiness independent.
    ☐ Implement sustained complete-coverage recovery and detail hysteresis. Fade only while current depths
      remain supplied and budgeted; unexpected loss goes unshadowed immediately, without stale-depth fading.
  ☐ Add counters/debug views for page requests/residency/validity, receiving coverage, candidate age and
    covered motion, unfinished traversal, overflow causes, source identity, selected detail, readiness,
    withdrawal/recovery, missing samples and baseline command/geometry cost.
  ☐ Delegate combined integration/failure checks for fresh visibility, transparent layers, motion within
    candidate coverage with depth invalidation, shared pages gaining receivers, overload, fair/stable
    recovery, memory shrink/format reset and in-flight work. Verify no CPU completion wait is introduced.
  ☐ Completion: current-frame readiness precedes every intended consumer, complete-light fallback and
    bounded work are verified, diagnostics explain failures and no partial prototype is enabled in
    production ahead of the coherent surface migration.

Phase 7: Migrate all participating surface lighting coherently
  Depends on Phase 6.
  Outcome: surface consumers use the shared virtual shadow system for simultaneous celestial and local
  lights, with one provider per light/consumer and complete current demand/readiness.

  ☐ Integrate shared shadow lookup and the same current light/readiness snapshot into deferred direct
    lighting, forward surfaces, entities/foliage, water/liquids and other participating transparent paths.
    ☐ Revalidate static sampler-bank capacity and supported configurations against each completed consumer's
      actual bindings; reject unsupported capacity rather than introducing a hidden optional extension.
    ☐ Update AtmosphereLighting publication, direct-light evaluation/intensity bounds and all forward
      consumers to retain independent solar and lunar contributions. Preserve calibration and storage units.
    ☐ Attenuate each light's direct term only; preserve ambient/emissive terms and unshadowed illumination
      for excluded lights. Keep illumination-list capacity independent from shadow admission settings.
    ☐ Exercise moving/carried lights as normal paths immediately, not as later stationary-cache extensions.
  ☐ Switch native/VSM ownership coherently for participating consumers and remove superseded sampling,
    shadow work and temporary integration scaffolding within the migrated scope.
    ☐ Audit native ShadowFar/ShadowNear scheduling and consumer bindings for duplicate work/attenuation.
      Retain native rendering only for explicitly selected remaining consumers, with recorded ownership;
      never use it as an undisclosed fallback for missing VSM data.
    ☐ Validate disabled/unconfigured behavior, source/dimension removal, world leave, context/resource
      recreation and shader/config reload without stale publications or leaked borrowed ownership.
  ☐ Delegate focused end-to-end surface integration and appropriate regressions, including all surface
    categories, two celestial sources, independent light failure, new receivers and transparent seams.
    ☐ Run canonical shader/build/package verification on the coherent migrated batch and reconcile package
      assets with the tested shaders/binary. Do not equate this evidence with live appearance/performance.
  ☐ Update permanent rendering/configuration documentation and source-to-consumer ownership records.
  ☐ Completion: every required surface path uses current consistent shadows with one provider; native
    migration, disabled behavior and resource restoration are verified. Runtime qualification remains
    explicitly open until Phase 8.

Phase 8: Qualify surface correctness, performance and visual behavior
  Depends on Phase 7.
  Outcome: the basic surface system meets agreed hardware-specific performance/quality conditions before
  full fog integration. A cheap frame caused by routinely withdrawing shadows cannot satisfy this gate.

  ☐ Delegate the complete relevant correctness/regression batch, retaining valid earlier scoped evidence.
    ☐ Cover baseline OpenGL 4.5 with optional paths disabled, representative capability limits, lifecycle
      and exhaustion, edits opening passages, streaming gaps, invalidating casters and coverage growth.
    ☐ Resolve any remaining near-source/depth-precision/material-opacity questions using actual geometry
      and image evidence; keep unproven connectivity pruning disabled with the bounds-only path operational.
  ☐ Collect attributable CPU/GPU p50/p95/p99 receipts under the Phase 1 combined dynamic workloads.
    ☐ Separate demand, publication, candidate maintenance, command generation, selective clear, depth and
      sampling cost. Record memory/retirement, false positives, repeated geometry, inactive slots and ready
      duty cycle; include initial fill, sustained motion, editing, streaming and recovery.
    ☐ Record hardware, capabilities, scene/configuration, executable/shader identity, sampling conditions
      and observed variability. Static cache-hit scenes are supplemental, not moving-light acceptance.
    ☐ Calibrate and document shipping profiles, finite limits and accepted quality/performance envelopes.
      If the baseline cannot meet them, keep this gate open and review changes within the proposal's
      constraints; do not silently add extensions, stale depths or caster-distance limits.
  ☐ Obtain user-run visual acceptance for simultaneous sun/moon, carried/moving lights, cutout/entity/
    foliage/water/transparent shadows, offscreen occluders, seams/contact bias, low angles, streaming
    edges, detail changes and whole-light withdrawal/recovery. Retain observations and limitations.
  ☐ Reconcile permanent docs, calibrated settings, diagnostics and qualification receipts; perform second
    review and independent audit before recording surface acceptance.
  ☐ Completion: correctness receipts, measured envelopes with useful shadow availability and user visual
    acceptance support the surface workload. Any unresolved required result stays open; full fog rendering
    has not been claimed or substituted for surface qualification.

Phase 9: Fully implement shadowed volumetric fog
  Depends on Phase 8. This is the final rendering-feature implementation phase.
  Outcome: volume lighting consumes the same pages with its own coverage, budgeted work, readiness and
  history validity, while preserving the qualified surface behavior.

  ☐ Locate the owning fog/volume lighting integration boundary and connect its actual sampling cells
    and filter footprints to shared demand without imposing broad coarse coverage on every light.
    ☐ Define and implement the fog resolution/coverage profile, including any justified coarse demand;
      use the existing light projections, source identities and candidate completeness rules.
    ☐ Schedule fog demand/page work before volume lighting, after giving surface coverage priority.
      Preserve current frame snapshots and charge volume-only work and metadata to shared budgets.
  ☐ Implement coverage-aware surface-page reuse and complete replacements for wider volume demand.
    ☐ Check receiver depth as well as page address; new associations cannot certify old depths. Replacements
      must retain all currently required surface coverage and respect in-flight resources/total memory.
    ☐ If a replacement cannot fit safely, defer fog expansion while keeping the complete surface result.
      Fog-only work must not evict required surface pages or withdraw surface shadows.
  ☐ Publish independent per-light fog readiness and apply shadows to each light's volume contribution.
    ☐ Missing fog coverage leaves that contribution unshadowed. Reject obsolete history on light/projection,
      page validity and readiness changes rather than accumulating old shadowing.
    ☐ Preserve simultaneous celestial/local behavior and the no-stale-depth policy under moving lights.
  ☐ Delegate GPU and integrated fog checks for volume coverage/filtering, shared-page depth differences,
    replacement exhaustion, surface preservation, history rejection, transitions and both light types.
  ☐ Measure the combined surface/fog workload with the same attribution and availability metrics; obtain
    user-run visual acceptance of fog shadows, temporal behavior and surface contention. Recheck affected
    surface acceptance and document the fog profile and evidence separately from prior surface receipts.
  ☐ Completion: full fog integration, independent readiness/history behavior, shared-budget contention
    and preserved surface results are verified and accepted; no further shadow feature is deferred to cleanup.

Phase 10: Final qualification, review and document cleanup
  Depends on Phase 9 and all preceding completion criteria.
  Outcome: the complete proposal is reconciled to qualified package artifacts, required evidence and permanent
  documentation, with temporary implementation-support material removed safely.

  ☐ Reconcile every row of the coverage map and every proposal requirement to implementation locations,
    tests, package evidence and runtime/visual receipts; retain each evidence type's limits.
    ☐ Keep required fog, moving-light, baseline capability, ownership and completeness work open if unmet.
      Any exceptional deferral needs explicit approved proposal reconciliation and rationale, not an
      unchecked runtime gap relabeled as finished work.
  ☐ Delegate final canonical build, relevant regression and package verification after the finished batch.
    ☐ Verify packaged binary and shader assets match tested outputs; reconcile skips/failures and preserve
      source/configuration/hardware identities. Repeat live checks only where changes invalidate evidence.
  ☐ Perform final second review and independent completion audit; correct confirmed findings as one batch
    and re-verify affected boundaries before completion. Record final user acceptance and remaining limits.
  ☐ Inventory temporary intermediate documents created solely to support implementation, including working
    notes, interim analyses and handoff drafts. No such document is designated for deletion at plan creation.
    ☐ Preserve still-required decisions/evidence in permanent destinations, update all references, then
      remove only the inventoried temporary implementation-support documents after scope/path checks.
    ☐ Retain Rendering.VirtualShadowMaps.Proposal.md, this plan, permanent rendering/configuration docs
      and required acceptance evidence. In particular, retain the referenced
      [chunk-spatial-inspection.txt](../artifacts/virtual-shadow-maps/chunk-spatial-inspection.txt) and
      [chunk-traversability-inspection.txt](../artifacts/virtual-shadow-maps/chunk-traversability-inspection.txt)
      receipts unless an explicitly reviewed evidence replacement preserves their required contents and links.
    ☐ Remove obsolete implementation-only scaffolding and superseded migrated artifacts after preserving
      needed checks; retain deliberate diagnostic/reference tooling and any explicitly owned native consumers.
  ☐ Update permanent documentation, effective configuration/defaults, qualification limitations and this
    plan's completion records. Validate links after cleanup and confirm no temporary compatibility path
    or hidden optional-capability dependency survived the migration.
  ☐ Completion: every required proposal behavior is implemented and qualified or explicitly reconciled
    through an approved scope change; canonical package checks, independent review, user acceptance,
    permanent documentation and temporary-document cleanup are complete with attributable evidence.
